    "evidence_text": "detection methods perform in a cross-lingual setting, as the multilingual datasets available today are relatively small, and specific to a single target , .\n\nThe dataset\n\nis split into a multilingual training set and into multiple test sets to evaluate zero-shot cross-lingual and cross-target transfer.\n\nIn addition, multilingual Bert can generalize to a degree both cross-lingually and in a cross-target setting.\n\nCrucially, the tweets in both languages focus on the same issue (Catalan independence); given this fact they are the first truly multilingual stance detection datasets known to us.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: Multilingual BERT Baseline ::: Results\tTable TABREF36 shows the results for the cross-lingual setting.\n\nFor example, the Italian test instances are combined with the Italian version of the questions, even though during training the model has only ever seen the German and French versions of the questions.\n\nWhile the effect is negligible in most settings, the cross-lingual performance clearly increases when all questions are\n\nOn the other hand, in the cross-lingual and cross-target settings, the mean score drops by 6\u20138 percentage points compared to the supervised setting; while zero-shot transfer is possible to a degree, it can still be improved.",
